<div class="blurb">
<div class="header"><h2>key facts</h2></div>
<div class="body">

<p>The framing above is concrete, but the numbers below put a scale on it.</p>

<ul>
<li>FDA considers MSG and DSG <strong>GRAS</strong> (Generally Recognized As Safe) <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a>, <a href="#src-fda-2">fda-2</a>, <a href="#src-fda-3">fda-3</a>, <a href="#src-fda-4">fda-4</a></sup></li>
<li>FDA threshold for symptoms: <strong>3 grams</strong> of MSG consumed without food (not per kg body weight) <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></li>
<li>Typical serving contains less than <strong>0.5 grams</strong> of added MSG <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></li>
<li>Average intake of added MSG: ~<strong>0.55 grams/day</strong> <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></li>
<li>Average total glutamate (from protein in food): ~<strong>13 grams/day</strong> <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></li>
<li>Glutamate sources that contribute to the free-glutamate load (whether the glutamate is added as a factory-made sodium salt, named on the label, or arrives via an ingredient that <em>carries</em> it naturally): added MSG and DSG; ingredients that contain free glutamate &mdash; yeast extract, hydrolyzed protein, autolyzed plant protein, soy sauce, fish sauce, aged cheeses, tomato paste, mushroom concentrate, yeast autolysate; nucleotide enhancers that amplify the umami effect when present with glutamate (disodium guanylate, disodium inosinate); and the umbrella label "natural flavor," which can legally hide a free-glutamate source. Finished dishes can also form MSG <em>in situ</em> from free-glutamate sources combined with sodium-bearing ingredients and heat &mdash; see <a href="#section-140-yeast-extract-and-sodium-can-form-msg-during-cooking">yeast extract and sodium can form MSG during cooking</a> for the in-situ loophole.</li>
</ul>
</div>
